9eac4993 pr_name is a literal name, and constrains nothing else
Three restrictions in the first cut were reasoned from what a shared pull
request might do to a sibling task's work, and none was asked for: a name
refused an automerge policy other than `nothing`, refused
`supersede_existing_pr`, and refused a task that opens no pull request. A
task's properties do not read each other. A run tries to auto-merge exactly
as it would without a name, and a policy that does not cover the accumulated
diff simply does not land it — which leaves the pull request open, where a
person can see it. That is the existing policy engine doing the job the
restrictions were invented for.

The pattern goes too: `pr_name` is a literal string, expanded by nothing and
matched against nothing. Two declarations share a pull request when they
carry the same name. With no pattern there is no calendar rotation, and with
it goes the ceiling the rotation was reasoned from — an open pull request
several tasks are still working on is alive, and when to close it is the
owner's call, not a clock's.

The PR-sense "lane" wording in the target seam goes with the concept. The
pre-existing senses stay: the agent and code delivery lanes, and the task's
own lane that one live item per task and `last-run-not-failed` hold — both
real mechanisms, keyed on the task name, not on a pull request.

